<Guide slug="webp-pdf-and-editable-projects">
  <h2>Choose the next task, then the file</h2>
  <p>
    There is no single best download for every job. Use WebP when you need an image inside another
    layout, PDF when you need a printable page, and a project file when you want to continue editing
    separate shapes. Keeping an editable project alongside the final export avoids having to rebuild
    a design later.
  </p>
  <div class="guide-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="File format comparison">
    <table>
      <caption>What UltraClipart's three file choices preserve</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Your next task</th>
          <th scope="col">Choose</th>
          <th scope="col">What to expect</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Place an illustration in a digital document</td>
          <td>WebP</td>
          <td>A raster image, with transparency when the background is transparent.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Print a page</td>
          <td>PDF</td>
          <td>
            A printable document. Catalog page sizes and raster or vector content vary by file.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Reopen the editable layers</td>
          <td>Save project</td>
          <td>An editor project file, not an image to place in a worksheet.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <h2>WebP: a finished image with a pixel limit</h2>
  <p>
    Image dimensions vary by artwork. Check Original resolution on the artwork page for the actual
    width and height. Files marked Transparent background let your document’s background show
    through. A checkerboard in the preview represents empty space; it is not printed into the image.
    Changing the preview background does not change the original download.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the editor, background changes do affect your export. Keep the background transparent if you
    want a cutout. After downloading, place the image over a colored rectangle in your destination
    app to check the edges. If that app cannot import WebP, use another workflow it explicitly
    supports; renaming the extension to .png does not convert the file. Google's{' '}
    <ContentLink href="https://developers.google.com/speed/webp?hl=en">
      WebP documentation
    </ContentLink>{' '}
    explains the format's transparency support.
  </p>
  <p>
    At four inches wide, a 2,048-pixel image has 512 pixels per inch. At eight inches, it has 256.
    These are calculations, not guarantees about a printer. Use the{' '}
    <a href="/print-size/">print size calculator</a> to explore your own dimensions without altering
    the file.
  </p>
  <h2>PDF: a print layout with specific color assumptions</h2>
  <p>
    Check the page dimensions listed for the catalog PDF you choose; not every download uses the
    same paper size. A PDF can contain raster artwork, vector artwork or both. An editor export
    follows your Print setup, including paper, orientation, margins and placement. Check the
    downloaded PDF’s page size in your viewer, and avoid applying an unintended second “fit”
    operation when printing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Vector studio exports use process CMYK shapes with the GRACoL2006 Coated1v2 output condition.
    Catalog PDFs may contain raster images instead of separate vector shapes. The standard file
    limit is 1,000,000 bytes. The full ICC profile is not embedded in these small PDFs; the file
    records an output condition. They are not certified PDF/X files. A print shop that needs a
    different profile, bleed, or a PDF/X standard may require additional preparation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Paper, ink, and printer settings affect the result. Bright screen colors can change in print,
    and CMYK does not mean every printer will produce an identical match. For a color-sensitive job,
    ask the printer about its requirements and request a proof. The{' '}
    <ContentLink href="https://archive.color.org/files/ICC_white_paper_23_RGB_Workflow.pdf">
      International Color Consortium's workflow guidance
    </ContentLink>{' '}
    explains why output conditions and destination profiles matter.
  </p>
  <h2>Project files: keep the decisions, not just the appearance</h2>
  <p>
    Use Save project before closing a design you may want to revise. Reopen it with Open saved
    project in the editor. That copy keeps editable layers and settings. WebP flattens the
    appearance into pixels, and importing a finished PDF back into editable layers is not supported
    here.
  </p>
  <p>
    Recovery is saved in the current browser when storage is available. It is not a cloud backup or
    a shared account. A different browser, cleared site data, or a private browsing session may not
    retain it. Download a project file to keep a separate copy, especially before opening another
    starter.
  </p>
  <h2>Check the final result</h2>
  <p>
    Open the exact file you downloaded. Confirm its background, crop, caption, and orientation. PDF
    captions use Helvetica-compatible Latin text; their appearance can differ slightly from the
    browser preview. Unsupported characters or effects may stop PDF export with an explanation. If
    you see an error, correct the source or choose a supported format rather than assuming a file
    was created.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="/license/">artwork license</a> applies to both formats and to edited versions of
    our illustrations. Changing the extension, color, or crop does not create commercial permission.
    Your own original work made on a blank canvas remains yours.
  </p>
</Guide>
